sbe1v1k: dts: describe the lan2/lan3 PHY LEDs (test)

Every port has two LEDs. lan2 and lan3 sit on the QCA8075 (PHYs 0x12
and 0x13), whose LED pins Linux leaves at the chip defaults today: at
1G both LEDs blink together. Describe them so qca807x registers them
and netdev rules can be offloaded to the PHY; the rules are set in
board.d/01_leds (green: 1G, yellow: 100M/10M, both blink on activity).

The stock QSDK 12.5 device tree has led_source@3/@6/@9 (mode "normal",
speed "all", blink enabled, active "high"). The SSDK maps a source to
port source / 3 + 1 and pin source % 3 (fal_led.c), so those are pin 0
of SSDK ports 2-4, i.e. PHYs 0x11-0x13; lan2 and lan3 are 0x12 and
0x13 here (0x11 is unused on this board). On QCA8075 (malibu_phy.c)
pin 0 is LED_100N (MMD7 0x8074) and pin 1 LED_1000N (0x8076), the same
registers qca807x uses for LED index 0 and 1.

Differences from the lan1 (QCA8081) nodes, on purpose:
 - led@0/led@1, not led@0/led@2: qca807x only accepts index 0 and 1
   and rejects anything else with -EINVAL;
 - no active-low/active-high: qca807x has no led_polarity_set, so
   either property makes of_phy_led() fail with -EINVAL, which fails
   the PHY probe and with it PPE ports 3 and 4. The pins keep the
   chip's polarity, which already lights them on link today.

Which colour is on which pin is not confirmed: if the board shows them
the other way round, swap LED_COLOR_ID_YELLOW and LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN
in both nodes (the 01_leds names follow the colour).

Signed-off-by: Crescentm <guyues@outlook.com>

diff --git a/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9570-sbe1v1k.dts b/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9570-sbe1v1k.dts
index baab57c524..caa5cf24c6 100644
--- a/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9570-sbe1v1k.dts
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9570-sbe1v1k.dts
@@ -571,11 +571,62 @@
 		qca8075_18: ethernet-phy@18 {
 			compatible = "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
 			reg = <18>;
+
+			/*
+			 * lan2 port LEDs. qca807x drives two LED pins per
+			 * PHY: reg 0 is the LED_100N pin (MMD7 0x8074, the one the
+			 * stock SSDK programs), reg 1 the LED_1000N pin (0x8076).
+			 * The colour per pin is not confirmed yet: swap the two
+			 * colors if the board shows them the other way round.
+			 * No active-low/active-high here: qca807x has no
+			 * led_polarity_set, and with either property the PHY
+			 * would fail to probe.
+			 */
+			leds {
+				#address-cells = <1>;
+				#size-cells = <0>;
+
+				led@0 {
+					reg = <0>;
+					color = <LED_COLOR_ID_YELLOW>;
+					default-state = "keep";
+					function = LED_FUNCTION_LAN;
+				};
+
+				led@1 {
+					reg = <1>;
+					color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+					default-state = "keep";
+					function = LED_FUNCTION_LAN;
+				};
+			};
 		};
 
 		qca8075_19: ethernet-phy@19 {
 			compatible = "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
 			reg = <19>;
+
+			/*
+			 * lan3 port LEDs, wired like lan2.
+			 */
+			leds {
+				#address-cells = <1>;
+				#size-cells = <0>;
+
+				led@0 {
+					reg = <0>;
+					color = <LED_COLOR_ID_YELLOW>;
+					default-state = "keep";
+					function = LED_FUNCTION_LAN;
+				};
+
+				led@1 {
+					reg = <1>;
+					color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+					default-state = "keep";
+					function = LED_FUNCTION_LAN;
+				};
+			};
 		};
 	};
 
